.TH SCANDUMP 8 2026-10-21 "scandump 2.3.0" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
scandump \- scan for Wi\-Fi networks and write the results in PCAP format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scandump
.RB [ \-c
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR interval ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR frequency\-list ]
.RB [ \-p ]
.I interface
.I filename
.br
.B scandump
.B \-h
|
.B \-V
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scandump
triggers a Wi\-Fi scan on
.I interface
through the Linux 802.11 netlink API (nl80211) and writes every network
(BSS) the kernel reports as a synthetic radiotap + IEEE 802.11 beacon
frame to
.IR filename ,
a PCAP file with link type
.BR DLT_IEEE802_11_RADIO .
If
.I filename
is
.BR \- ,
the PCAP stream is written to standard output.
Each scan's frames are written together, and flushed, once all of its
results have been read, so the output only ever holds complete scans.
.PP
Each frame carries the BSSID, channel frequency and band, signal strength,
beacon timestamp (TSF), beacon interval, capability information and the
information elements reported by the driver.
The interface does not need to support monitor mode, and the output can be
read with any PCAP\-aware tool, such as
.BR tcpdump (8)
or Wireshark, instead of parsing the text output of
.BR iw (8).
.PP
By default
.B scandump
scans repeatedly until it is interrupted with
.B SIGINT
or
.BR SIGTERM ,
at which point the output file is closed cleanly, or until the program
reading its standard output closes the pipe.
The output file is created only after the first scan succeeds.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-count " \fIcount\fR"
Exit after
.I count
scans.
A count of 0 (the default) scans until interrupted.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interval " \fIinterval\fR"
Wait
.I interval
seconds between scans, a whole number from 0 to 86400.
The wait starts when a scan's results have been written and ends when the
next scan starts; there is none before the first scan or after the last.
It is measured on the monotonic clock, so changes to the system time do not
affect it, and
.B SIGINT
or
.B SIGTERM
ends it immediately.
The default, 0, starts the next scan right away.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-frequency " \fIfrequency\-list\fR"
Restrict the scan to a comma\-separated list of center frequencies in MHz,
for example
.BR 2412,5180 .
Up to 100 distinct frequencies may be given.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-passive
Perform a passive scan: listen for beacons without transmitting probe
requests.
The default is an active (wildcard SSID) scan.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH PRIVILEGES
Triggering a scan requires the
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
capability; reading scan results does not.
Run
.B scandump
as root (for example with
.BR sudo (8)),
or grant the capability to the binary:
.PP
.RS
.B setcap cap_net_admin+ep /usr/bin/scandump
.RE
.PP
With a file capability, any local user can trigger scans and the output
file is created with the calling user's privileges.
Installing the package grants no privileges by itself.
.PP
When run as root,
.B scandump
writes to whatever path it is given, so do not grant passwordless
.BR sudo (8)
access to it for users who should not be able to overwrite files as root.
An example sudoers rule is shipped with the documentation for deployments
that accept this.
.PP
Without the required privilege the scan request fails with
.B Operation not permitted
and
.B scandump
exits with a non\-zero status.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Transient scan failures (for example, the interface is busy because another
process is scanning, or the scan results could not be read) are retried
every 2 seconds, up to 30 times in a row; a scan that failed is not counted
towards
.BR \-c .
Errors are reported on standard error, followed by a hint for the common
ones.
.TP
.B 0
The requested number of scans completed, or
.B scandump
was interrupted, or the program reading its standard output closed the
pipe.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments, or no interface with the given name.
.TP
.B 240
The scan kept failing transiently (the interface stayed busy, or the
driver did not respond) and
.B scandump
gave up.
.TP
.B 255
Any other error, such as an output file that cannot be created or written.
.TP
.I "256 \- N"
A scan failed with netlink error
.IR N ,
the number shown in parentheses in the error message.
For example, 225 (\(lqNo such device (\-31)\(rq) means the interface does
not support nl80211, and 228 (\(lqOperation not permitted (\-28)\(rq)
means scandump lacks
.BR CAP_NET_ADMIN .
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan on
.B wlan0
and save the results to
.BR scan.pcap :
.PP
.RS
.B sudo scandump wlan0 scan.pcap
.RE
.PP
Stream a single scan to
.BR tcpdump (8):
.PP
.RS
.B sudo scandump \-c 1 wlan0 \- | tcpdump \-r \- \-e
.RE
.PP
Scan only channels 1 (2.4 GHz) and 36 (5 GHz):
.PP
.RS
.B sudo scandump \-f 2412,5180 wlan0 scan.pcap
.RE
.PP
Scan passively, five times:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo scandump \-\-passive \-c 5 wlan0 scan.pcap
.RE
.PP
Scan ten times, waiting five seconds between scans:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo scandump \-\-count 10 \-\-interval 5 wlan0 scans.pcap
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B scandump
requires a Linux kernel with nl80211 (cfg80211) support and a wireless
driver that implements scanning through it.
Scan results are synthesized from the kernel's BSS table, not captured
over the air.
Every frame is written as a beacon carrying all of the information
elements the kernel reports, however large, together with the TSF
(802.11 timestamp field) of the frame they came from.
After an active scan that is usually a probe response, which, unlike a
beacon, has no TIM element and carries the SSID of a hidden network; beacon
and probe response data are never mixed in one frame.
The destination is always the broadcast address, the sequence number and
duration are 0, and there is no FCS.
Each scan writes every network in the kernel's scan results, the same table
.B iw dev
.I interface
.B scan
shows, not only the networks that scan heard.
The kernel keeps a network there for about 30 seconds after it last
received a frame from it (longer for the network the interface is connected
to), whichever process's scan or connection received that frame.
A scan can therefore include networks it missed and, with
.BR \-f ,
networks on other channels.
.B scandump
itself keeps no results between scans.
The PCAP timestamp is when the results were written, not when the frame was
received, so that records are always in time order; a network the kernel
still reports from an earlier frame is written with each scan's time.
The signal is rounded toward zero to whole dBm; drivers that do not report
it in dBm get no signal field.
Networks reported without information elements are skipped with a warning.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iw (8),
.BR tcpdump (8),
.BR pcap (3PCAP),
.BR capabilities (7),
.BR setcap (8)
